course: dictionary
lesson: 7245
title: Picky: hard to please
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Picky is an informal word. A picky person is hard to please, and only likes certain things. «That's why a picky grandad says the new millennium really began in 2001, not at the huge parties of 2000.» The grandad will not accept the answer everyone celebrates. He wants his own answer, so he is hard to please.
explain: Picky can also mean fussing about small details. You might hear «a picky eater», someone who only eats a few foods, or «picky about clothes». Notice the clues around the word: the grandad turns down the big party year, which shows he is fussy about exact details.
explain: Picky can sound a bit critical, so it is not always a kind word. In the UK, people often say fussy or faddy instead. Test your meaning in the sentence: «a fussy grandad says the new millennium really began in 2001» still makes sense, so fussy is a good swap here.
fact: Picky is informal, so you use it in chatty speech and writing, not in formal reports.
fact: «Picky» means hard to please and only liking certain things.
fact: «Picky» can also mean fussing about small details, as in «picky about clothes».
fact: Picky has forms: picky, pickier, pickiest.
fact: Synonyms: fussy and faddy (UK) have similar meanings, but picky is the everyday informal choice.
exercise: «That's why a picky grandad says the new millennium really began in 2001.» What does picky mean here? | Quick to laugh | *Hard to please, and only accepts one answer | Happy to celebrate with everyone | Very old and wise
exercise: Ella says, «My little brother is a picky eater.» What does picky mean? | *Only likes certain foods | Never eats at all | Eats very fast | Cooks dinner for everyone
exercise: Which word means almost the same as picky in the UK? | *Fussy | Barely | Gigantic | Luminous
test:
Mum says, «You are being picky about your clothes today.» What does picky mean? | *Fussing about small details | Dancing happily | Running very fast | Asleep in bed
Which is the comparative form of picky, for comparing two people? | more picky | *pickier | pickiest | picker
exam: Which sentence uses picky correctly and informally? | *My cousin is so picky that he only eats plain pasta. | The picky sum equals twelve. | The picky mountain rose above the clouds. | The committee's picky report was approved at the formal meeting.
